ust a huge amount of money but  governments around the world still  struggling to find that 100 billion  which is a lot smaller a year for  vulnerable countries  that's an exceptionally good question  and just to support what jen was just  saying the plans are not smart they're  not specific measurable accurate  relevant time related that's because  they're new um they haven't been adopted  by cop but this starts the conversation  and you're absolutely right um being  able to have funds available for for  loss and adaptation is an absolutely  essential uh a step that has to be taken  the hundred billion that was promised  hasn't been reached but it's so much  lower than where we need to be so again  at the conference exchange plan we did  our numbers and we came up with 2.2  trillion per annum and luckily people  like mark carney have come out and said  that number is even too low we need to  go even further so i think we're  actually in a stage of negotiations  where 2050 will be challenged we need  2030. funds will become available both  from government and from private sector  this is the beginning of a really  interesting change we are potentially  turning the titanic around so yes i'll  absolutely admit this is just a start  but it's an exciting one  okay uh good to end on some positivity  there but just before we go jen i want  to bring in the thoughts of sarah jane  ahmed she's a climate finance advisor  for vulnerable countries that you can  see what she had to say at cop she said  the message coming out of this cop is  every country for themselves now that is  pretty damning jen i mean would you  agree with that  yeah finance at the last cop was really  difficult the fact that developed  countries couldn't make good on a  ten-year-old promise for a hundred  billion just dismantled any trust that  there was between countries  and the fact that they couldn't talk  about finance for the permanent effects  of climate change for permanent losses  and damages  th